 scripture:[["John 4:24, adore him in spirit and in truth",null],["Matthew 6:7, speak not much, as the heathens",null],["Luke 17:17–18, where are the nine?",null],["Acts 5:29, we ought to obey God rather than men",null],["Romans 13:7, honour, to whom honour",null]],
 sections:[
 {id:"s1",h:"1. Debts we can never fully pay: the potential parts of justice",html:`<p>Justice requires two things: that something be owed to another, and that equality be possible. Where equality is impossible, we have <strong>virtues annexed to justice</strong> (q.80):</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Virtue</th><th>Owed to</th><th>Why not strict justice</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Religion</td><td>God</td><td>We can never repay God equally</td></tr>
<tr><td>Piety</td><td>Parents and country</td><td>We can never repay the gift of life and upbringing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Observance (dulia, obedience)</td><td>Those excelling in dignity or authority</td><td>A debt of honour</td></tr>
<tr><td>Truthfulness, gratitude, vindication, liberality, affability</td><td>Others generally</td><td>A moral, not legal, debt</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
${RC("t8tr1",`<p>Truthfulness 1: truthfulness is one of these annexed virtues, owed as a moral debt for the sake of social life.</p>`)}`,
  check:Q("Why is religion only annexed to justice, not strict justice?",[["It is not directed to another","It is."],["We can never render to God an equal return","Correct."],["It is a theological virtue","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s2",h:"2. Religion: a moral virtue with God as end",html:`<div class="quote">"Hence it is evident that God is related to religion not as matter or object, but as end: and consequently religion is not a theological virtue whose object is the last end, but a moral virtue which is properly about things referred to the end."<span class="src">ST II-II q.81 a.5 · Dominican tr. (1920)</span></div>
<p>Faith believes God; religion <em>worships</em> God by acts done out of reverence. Thomas calls it the highest of the moral virtues because it comes nearest to God (q.81 a.6). Worship is not for God's benefit but ours: we honour God not because He needs it but because, by reverencing Him, our mind is subjected to Him and so perfected (q.81 a.7, paraphrased).</p>
<p><strong>Interior acts</strong>: devotion and prayer. <strong>Exterior acts</strong>: adoration (bodily reverence), sacrifice, oblations, tithes, vows, oaths, and invocation of God's name.</p>
<div class="quote">"…devotion is apparently nothing else but the will to give oneself readily to things concerning the service of God."<span class="src">ST II-II q.82 a.1 · Dominican tr. (1920)</span></div>
<p>Note: devotion is a <strong>readiness of will</strong>, not a feeling. A person in spiritual dryness, who still prays faithfully, has devotion.</p>`,
  check:Q("Devotion is, for Thomas…",[["intense religious feeling","No."],["the will to give oneself readily to the service of God","Correct: q.82 a.1."],["a vow","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s3",h:"3. Why pray, if God's providence does not change?",html:`<p>Thomas names three errors that make prayer pointless: that God does not govern human affairs; that everything happens by necessity; that God's disposition can be changed by our prayers (q.83 a.2).</p>
<div class="quote">"For we pray not that we may change the Divine disposition, but that we may impetrate that which God has disposed to be fulfilled by our prayers in other words 'that by asking, men may deserve to receive what Almighty God from eternity has disposed to give,' as Gregory says…"<span class="src">ST II-II q.83 a.2 · Dominican tr. (1920)</span></div>
<p>Analogy: God wills that plants grow by rain and soil; He also wills certain goods to come by prayer. Prayer is a <strong>secondary cause</strong> that God freely includes in his plan, giving us the dignity of causality (I q.22 a.3). And prayer changes the one who prays: it disposes us to receive.</p>
<p><strong>Superstition and irreligion</strong> (q.92): superstition gives worship to whom it is not due (idolatry) or in an undue manner (e.g. treating a prayer formula as a magic guarantee, divination, consulting horoscopes or muti for hidden knowledge). Irreligion fails by defect: tempting God, perjury, sacrilege, simony. Jesus warns against magical repetition: "speak not much, as the heathens" (Matt 6:7).</p>
${RC("t9a",`<p>T9a: the kinds and degrees of prayer, from vocal to contemplative.</p>`)}`,
  check:Q("Prayer works, for Thomas, because…",[["it changes God's mind","No."],["God disposes some goods to come through our prayers as secondary causes","Correct: q.83 a.2."],["it is magic","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s4",h:"4. Piety, obedience, gratitude",html:`<div class="quote">"Consequently man is debtor chiefly to his parents and his country, after God. Wherefore just as it belongs to religion to give worship to God, so does it belong to piety, in the second place, to give worship to one's parents and one's country."<span class="src">ST II-II q.101 a.1 · Dominican tr. (1920)</span></div>
<ul><li><strong>Piety</strong> includes care for parents in need, honour, and love of country (patriotism, not nationalism). It never overrides religion: if parents draw one from God, "we ought to obey God rather than men" (Acts 5:29; q.101 a.4).</li>
<li><strong>Obedience</strong> (q.104) is due to superiors within the sphere of their authority. Subjects are not bound to obey (a) against God's command, or (b) in matters outside the superior's competence (e.g. inner thoughts, choice of state of life) (q.104 a.5). Blind obedience to evil is not a virtue: the defence 'I was following orders' fails.</li>
<li><strong>Gratitude</strong> (q.106) follows the order of debts: God, parents, those excelling in dignity, benefactors. It has degrees: recognising the favour, giving thanks, and repaying as one can. Of the ten lepers, only one returned: "Were not ten made clean? And where are the nine?" (Luke 17:17).</li></ul>
<div class="co core"><div class="co-h">[Core] Davis et al. (2016), J Couns Psychol 63(1), 20–31</div><p>Meta-analyses of gratitude interventions (k = 4–20; N = 395–1,755). Versus measurement-only controls: psychological well-being d = 0.31. Versus alternative activities: gratitude d = 0.46, well-being d = 0.17, anxiety d = 0.11 (non-significant). Versus psychologically active comparisons: no difference. <strong>Interpretation</strong>: gratitude exercises help modestly; they are not a substitute for treatment of depression or anxiety. doi:10.1037/cou0000107.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Are subjects bound to obey a superior's command against God's law?",[["Yes","No."],["No: we ought to obey God rather than men","Correct: q.104 a.5."],["Only in emergencies","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s5",h:"5. Worship as justice: the Sunday obligation and daily life",html:`<p>The Sunday obligation is not an arbitrary rule but a determination, by the Church, of the natural duty of religion: humans owe God public worship at set times (q.122 a.4; CCC 2176–2185). Missing Mass deliberately without grave reason is a serious sin against religion (CCC 2181); illness, care of infants, or serious impediment excuse.</p>
<div class="co apply"><div class="co-h">A just person's week</div><ul><li><strong>Religion</strong>: Sunday Mass; daily prayer, however short; the Hours if possible.</li>
<li><strong>Piety</strong>: regular contact with parents; prayer for South Africa and its leaders (1 Tim 2:1–2).</li>
<li><strong>Observance</strong>: courtesy to lecturers, priests, employers, even when disagreeing.</li>
<li><strong>Gratitude</strong>: thank someone specific each day; an evening examen beginning with thanksgiving.</li></ul></div>
${RC("p4l4",`<p>P4 L4: religion in therapy. Clients' religious practice can be a resource; superstitious or scrupulous practice may need careful, respectful work.</p>`)}`,
  check:Q("Is the Sunday obligation merely a Church rule?",[["Yes","No."],["It determines the natural duty of public worship owed to God","Correct."],["It applies only to clergy","No."]],1)}
 ],
